      "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = \\dfrac{2-x}{x+3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find $f^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{2-x}{x+3}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{2-y}{y+3}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2-y}{y+3} \\\\ x(y+3) & =2-y \\\\ xy+3x & =2-y \\\\ xy+y & =2-3x \\\\ y(x+1) & =2-3x \\\\ y & =\\frac{2-3x}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{2-3x}{x+1}.\n$$",
